// PARAM: --analysis interval --solver warrow
// FM 2026 Example 2: the paper's Fig. 1 analyzed with intervals and
// widening, where the three contributions to the global g are accumulated
// into one value that is widened on every update.
//
//   [1,1]                       the initializer's contribution
//   [1,1]    \/  [-17,1]        joined with main's -17; the lower bound
//                               grew downwards, so widening drops it
//            ->  [-inf,1]
//   [-inf,1] \/  [-inf,42]      joined with f's 42; the upper bound grew,
//            ->  [-inf,+inf]    so widening drops that too
//
// so the paper fails to deduce g <= 42 and cannot rule out the error call.
//
// Voblint analyzes declared globals flow-sensitively (19-paper-examples/
// precision/01-fig1_sequential_globals.vimp answers Fig. 1 exactly), so the
// loss cannot be staged on a `global`. It is reproduced here on the one
// unknown Voblint does treat flow-insensitively with several contributing
// origins: a procedure's FunctionEntry seed, side-effected once per call
// site. Three call sites contribute the paper's own three values, in the
// paper's order, and Apinis warrowing walks the identical chain to
// [-inf,+inf] -- so x < 43 comes back UNKNOWN even though x is concretely
// one of 1, -17, 42.
//
// Mechanism, stated as the convention requires: the update rule widens the
// slot's already-joined value against each new contribution. No single
// origin's own contribution ever moves -- each call site passes one
// constant -- so both bounds are lost purely to joining across origins
// before widening. 03-per_origin_widening.vimp is the same program under
// the update rule that fixes exactly this.

void f(int32 x) {
  __voblint_check(x < 43) // UNKNOWN
}
void main() {
  f(1);
  f(-17);
  f(42)
}
